---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Header client:load />

  <main class="post-main">

    <section class="post-hero">
      <div class="post-container">
        <nav class="post-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
          <a href="/blog/">Blog</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
          <span>GST Invoice Guide 2026</span>
        </nav>
        <div class="post-meta-top">
          <span class="post-tag">GST Billing</span>
          <span class="post-date">August 20, 2026</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="post-h1">GST Invoice for Restaurant and Dhaba in India — Step by Step Guide 2026</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">Every GST-registered restaurant must issue compliant tax invoices — but getting the format, rate split, or required fields wrong creates audit risk. This guide covers everything you need.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="post-content-section">
      <div class="post-container">
        <div class="post-body">

          <p>If your restaurant, dhaba, or food counter is registered under GST, issuing proper tax invoices is a legal requirement — not optional. Getting it wrong doesn't just inconvenience your business customers who need to claim input tax credit. It creates mismatches in your GSTR-1 filing and flags your account for scrutiny during audits.</p>
          <p>This guide explains what a valid restaurant GST invoice must contain, which rate applies to food service businesses, and how billing software eliminates the manual errors that create these problems.</p>

          <h2>Does Your Restaurant Need to Issue GST Invoices?</h2>
          <p>You are required to issue GST-compliant tax invoices if your restaurant is registered under the regular GST scheme. Registration becomes mandatory when annual turnover exceeds ₹20 lakh (for most states) or ₹10 lakh (for special category states).</p>
          <p>If you are under the <strong>Composition Scheme</strong> — available for restaurants with turnover up to ₹1.5 crore — you cannot issue a tax invoice. You must issue a <strong>Bill of Supply</strong> instead and cannot collect GST from customers. You pay the tax yourself at 5% of turnover.</p>
          <p>If you are not GST-registered at all (turnover below the threshold), no GST invoice is required.</p>

          <h2>GST Rate for Restaurants and Dhabas in India</h2>
          <p>Most standalone restaurants in India — regardless of whether they have air conditioning — pay <strong>5% GST</strong> on food and beverage revenue. This breaks down as <strong>2.5% CGST + 2.5% SGST</strong> for intra-state supplies. Input Tax Credit (ITC) is not available at this rate.</p>
          <p>A higher rate of <strong>18% GST</strong> (9% CGST + 9% SGST) applies only to restaurants inside hotels where the declared room tariff exceeds ₹7,500 per night. These establishments can claim ITC.</p>
          <p>For dhabas: the 5% rate applies. Highway dhabas, city dhabas, and roadside eateries all fall under this category.</p>
          <p>The <strong>SAC code for restaurant services is 9963</strong> — this must appear on every tax invoice you issue.</p>
          <p class="post-note">⚠️ GST Council revises rates periodically. Verify current rates with your chartered accountant or the GST portal before filing returns.</p>

          <h2>Mandatory Fields in a Restaurant GST Invoice</h2>
          <p>A valid tax invoice for a restaurant must contain all of the following:</p>
          <ol class="post-ol">
            <li><strong>"TAX INVOICE" heading</strong> — Must appear clearly at the top of the document</li>
            <li><strong>Supplier details</strong> — Your restaurant's legal name, registered address, and GSTIN</li>
            <li><strong>Invoice number</strong> — Unique and sequential; restart the sequence each financial year (April 1)</li>
            <li><strong>Invoice date</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Customer details</strong> — Name and address. For GST-registered business customers: their GSTIN is required so they can claim input tax credit</li>
            <li><strong>SAC code: 9963</strong> — Service Accounting Code for restaurant services</li>
            <li><strong>Description of supply</strong> — Item names and quantities ordered</li>
            <li><strong>Taxable value</strong> — Total amount before GST is applied</li>
            <li><strong>CGST amount</strong> — 2.5% of taxable value (at 5% rate)</li>
            <li><strong>SGST amount</strong> — 2.5% of taxable value (at 5% rate). If the customer is in a different state, replace CGST + SGST with IGST at 5%</li>
            <li><strong>Total invoice amount</strong> — Taxable value plus GST</li>
            <li><strong>Reverse charge declaration</strong> — "Whether tax is payable on reverse charge: No" for standard restaurant transactions</li>
          </ol>

          <h2>Common GST Billing Mistakes Restaurants Make</h2>
          <p>These errors are common in manual billing setups and create problems during GSTR-1 filing or GST audits:</p>

          <div class="post-mistake">
            <h3>1. Duplicate invoice numbers</h3>
            <p>Issuing the same invoice number twice creates mismatches in GSTR-1. The portal reconciles your returns against your customers' GSTR-2B — duplicates trigger errors and notices. Software that auto-increments invoice numbers eliminates this entirely.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-mistake">
            <h3>2. Writing "5% GST" without the CGST/SGST breakup</h3>
            <p>The split — 2.5% CGST and 2.5% SGST — must appear separately on the invoice. A single line saying "GST: 5%" is technically non-compliant for a formal tax invoice, even if the total amount is correct.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-mistake">
            <h3>3. Missing customer GSTIN for business buyers</h3>
            <p>When a corporate client, hotel, or catering company buys from your restaurant and wants to claim ITC, they need their GSTIN to appear on your invoice. Without it, their credit claim fails — and you lose a business customer.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-mistake">
            <h3>4. SAC code missing from the invoice</h3>
            <p>Many restaurants skip the SAC code field on handwritten or basic printed bills. It is legally required on tax invoices for service providers.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-mistake">
            <h3>5. Manual arithmetic errors</h3>
            <p>Calculating 2.5% of ₹847 by hand, across dozens of bills per day, inevitably produces rounding and multiplication mistakes. These accumulate into discrepancies in your returns.</p>
          </div>

          <h2>Composition Scheme Restaurants — What's Different</h2>
          <p>If your restaurant operates under the Composition Scheme:</p>
          <ul class="post-ul">
            <li>Issue a <strong>Bill of Supply</strong>, not a Tax Invoice</li>
            <li>The bill must contain the mandatory text: <em>"Composition taxable person, not eligible to collect tax on supplies"</em></li>
            <li>You cannot charge GST separately from the customer — your 5% composition tax is paid out of your own revenue</li>
            <li>Your billing software should support a Composition mode that generates the correct bill format</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>How Billing Software Handles GST Invoices</h2>
          <p>Manual GST invoicing has too many moving parts for a busy restaurant counter — maintaining sequential numbers, calculating CGST/SGST correctly for every bill, getting the right fields into the right format. One systemic error repeated across 50 bills a day becomes a filing problem.</p>
          <p>POS billing software automates the entire process:</p>
          <ul class="post-ul">
            <li>Your GSTIN and business details are entered once and appear on every invoice automatically</li>
            <li>CGST + SGST are calculated at the correct rate — no manual arithmetic</li>
            <li>Invoice numbers auto-increment — duplicate numbers become impossible</li>
            <li>The "TAX INVOICE" header appears automatically for the correct transaction type</li>
            <li>Business customers can have their GSTIN entered at billing time — it appears on the invoice immediately</li>
            <li>Bills can be printed on Bluetooth thermal paper or shared on WhatsApp in one tap</li>
          </ul>
          <p><a href="/restaurant-pos-software/" class="post-link">Zipla POS</a> handles GST invoice generation for <a href="/dhaba-billing-software/" class="post-link">dhabas</a>, restaurants, <a href="/cafe-pos-software/" class="post-link">cafes</a>, and <a href="/qsr-billing-software/" class="post-link">QSR counters</a>. The TAX INVOICE header, GSTIN field, and CGST/SGST breakup are built into the billing flow. When a commercial customer needs a proper tax invoice, you add their GSTIN at the counter and the invoice generates correctly in one step — no separate module, no manual formatting.</p>

          <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="post-faq">

            <div class="post-faq-item">
              <h3>Does a customer have to ask for a GST invoice, or does the restaurant have to issue it automatically?</h3>
              <p>For registered business customers purchasing above ₹200, issuing a tax invoice is mandatory regardless of whether they ask. For walk-in retail customers, a simplified invoice is acceptable unless the customer specifically requests a full tax invoice.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-faq-item">
              <h3>What if the customer doesn't have a GSTIN?</h3>
              <p>Leave the GSTIN field blank or mark "Unregistered." The customer's name and address are sufficient for retail transactions. Individual consumers cannot claim ITC, so GSTIN is not relevant for them.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-faq-item">
              <h3>Can a highway dhaba issue GST invoices to truck drivers or transport companies?</h3>
              <p>Yes — if the dhaba is GST-registered and the truck driver's employer is also a registered business, they can provide their GSTIN and receive a full tax invoice. This allows the business to claim ITC on their travel expenses.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-faq-item">
              <h3>How long do restaurants need to keep GST invoice records?</h3>
              <p>GST law requires records to be maintained for 72 months (6 years) from the due date of the relevant annual return. Digital records stored in billing software count.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-faq-item">
              <h3>We don't always have internet at our dhaba. Can we still generate GST bills?</h3>
              <p>Zipla POS works in partial offline mode — bills can be created after the app menu loads, even without an active connection. UPI payments require internet. GST invoices are generated locally and can be printed on a paired Bluetooth thermal printer without internet.</p>
            </div>

          </div>

          <div class="post-related">
            <h2>Related Guides</h2>
            <p>Zipla POS billing guides for Indian food businesses: <a href="/restaurant-pos-software/">Restaurant POS Software</a>, <a href="/dhaba-billing-software/">Dhaba Billing Software</a>, <a href="/cafe-pos-software/">Cafe POS Software</a>, <a href="/qsr-billing-software/">QSR Billing Software</a>, <a href="/bakery-billing-software/">Bakery Billing Software</a>.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-cta">
            <h2>GST-Compliant Billing for Your Restaurant — Starting ₹2,999/Year</h2>
            <p>Zipla POS generates proper TAX INVOICE format with GSTIN, CGST/SGST breakup, and auto-sequential invoice numbers — from any Android phone. 14-day free trial, no credit card required.</p>
            <a href="https://app.zipla.in/register" class="post-btn-primary">Start Free Trial</a>
            <a href="/#pricing" class="post-btn-secondary">See Pricing</a>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

